Thread GB: does genericity make mistakes cheap to locate?  (python3 -B threadGB_location.py)

== E1: location under three random-program classes (exact Bayes; chain-equality verifier, n = n0) ==
   T = 300 problems per human; 12 humans per row (drawn from the class prior unless marked)
   Columns: rej = rejections per human; Q = task queries per human; Q/rej = mean search cost per rejection (max);
   at the same rejections, before the search: G = guessing cost of lambda, 1/maxL, 2^H, Ar = (sum sqrt lambda)^2,
   supp = |supp lambda|, susp = suspect steps (doubt > 0), f+1 = scan position of the true first mistake,
   rank = its rank in the lambda order; for CI: wrong = wrong outputs per human (CI makes T queries).
   thr bound = Theorem GB2: K (1 + 1/c + n/log2(1/gamma)), gamma = log2(n)/n, c = log2(1/(1-gamma)).

-- M1 prior, n = 16 --
   | protocol | rej | Q | Q/rej (max) | G | 1/maxL | 2^H | Ar | supp | susp | f+1 | rank | wrong (CI) | K | thr bound |
   |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   | CI | - | 300 | (15.3 queries per hit) | - | - | - | - | - | - | - | - | 113.2 | 96 | - |
   | scan | 27.8 | 221.1 | 7.97 (16) | 1.12 | 1.1 | 1.4 | 1.6 | 2.9 | 3.8 | 8.0 | 1.1 | 0 | 96 | - |
   | bayes | 27.8 | 30.7 | 1.11 (3) | 1.12 | 1.1 | 1.4 | 1.6 | 2.9 | 3.8 | 8.0 | 1.1 | 0 | 96 | - |
   | thr | 26.3 | 27.8 | 1.06 (3) | 1.11 | 1.1 | 1.4 | 1.6 | 2.8 | 3.8 | 7.6 | 1.1 | 0 | 96 | 1095 (max Q 33; fallbacks 0.0) |

